@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.54}Exercise 3.54:} Define a function
@code{mul_streams}, analogous to @code{add_streams}, that produces the
elementwise product of its two input streams.  Use this together with the
stream of @code{integers} to complete the following definition of the stream
whose @math{n^{\hbox{th}}} element (counting from 0) is @math{{n + 1}} factorial:

@example ocaml
let factorials = cons_stream 1 (mul_streams ⟨??⟩ ⟨??⟩)
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.55}Exercise 3.55:} Define a procedure
@code{partial_sums} that takes as argument a stream @math{S} and returns the
stream whose elements are @math{S_0}, @math{{S_0 + S_1}}, @math{{S_0 + S_1} + {S_2, \dots}}.  
For example, @code{partial_sums integers} should be the
stream 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, @dots{}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.56}Exercise 3.56:} A famous problem, first raised by
R. Hamming, is to enumerate, in ascending order with no repetitions, all
positive integers with no prime factors other than 2, 3, or 5.  One obvious way
to do this is to simply test each integer in turn to see whether it has any
factors other than 2, 3, and 5.  But this is very inefficient, since, as the
integers get larger, fewer and fewer of them fit the requirement.  As an
alternative, let us call the required stream of numbers @code{s} and notice the
following facts about it.

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{s} begins with 1.

@item
The elements of @code{scale_stream s 2} are also
elements of @code{s}.

@item
The same is true for @code{scale_stream s 3}
and @code{scale_stream s 5}.

@item
These are all the elements of @code{s}.

@end itemize

Now all we have to do is combine elements from these sources.  For this we
define a procedure @code{merge} that combines two ordered streams into one
ordered result stream, eliminating repetitions:

@example ocaml
let rec merge s1 s2 =
  match stream_null s1, stream_null s2 with
  | true, _ -> s2
  | _, true -> s1
  | _ ->
    let s1car = stream_car s1 in
    let s2car = stream_car s2 in
    if s1car < s2car
    then
      cons_stream s1car (fun () -> merge (stream_cdr s1) s2)
    else if s1car > s2car
    then cons_stream s2car (fun () -> merge s1 (stream_cdr s2))
    else
      cons_stream
        s1car
        (fun () -> merge (stream_cdr s1) (stream_cdr s2))
@end example

Then the required stream may be constructed with @code{merge}, as follows:

@example ocaml
let rec s = Cons (1, lazy (merge ⟨??⟩ ⟨??⟩))
@end example

Fill in the missing expressions in the places marked @code{⟨??⟩} above.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.57}Exercise 3.57:} How many additions are performed
when we compute the @math{n^{\hbox{th}}} Fibonacci number using the definition of
@code{fibs} based on the @code{add_streams} procedure?  Show that the number of
additions would be exponentially greater if the tails had been plain thunks
@code{fun () -> ⟨@var{exp}⟩}, without the
memoization that @code{lazy} and the @code{memo_proc} of
@ref{3.5.1} provide.@footnote{This exercise shows how call-by-need is closely related
to ordinary memoization as described in @ref{Exercise 3.27}.  In that exercise,
we used assignment to explicitly construct a local table.  Our call-by-need
stream optimization effectively constructs such a table automatically, storing
values in the previously forced parts of the stream.}
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.58}Exercise 3.58:} Give an interpretation of the
stream computed by the following procedure:

@example ocaml
let rec expand num den radix =
  cons_stream
    (num * radix / den)
    (fun () -> expand ((num * radix) mod den) den radix)
@end example

(@code{/} on integers is the quotient here, and @code{mod} the remainder.)
What are the successive elements produced by @code{expand 1 7
10}?  What is produced by @code{expand 3 8 10}?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.59}Exercise 3.59:} In @ref{2.5.3} we saw how
to implement a polynomial arithmetic system representing polynomials as lists
of terms.  In a similar way, we can work with @newterm{power series}, such as
@displaymath
\eqalign{
  e^x 	  &= 1 + x + {1 \over 2} x^2  + {1 \over 3 \cdot 2} x^3  + {1 \over 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2} x^4  + \dots, \cr
  \cos x  &= 1 - {1 \over 2} x^2  + {1 \over 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2} x^4  - \dots, \cr
  \sin x  &= x - {1 \over 3 \cdot 2} x^3  + {1 \over 5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2} x^5  - \dots
}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
               x^2     x^3       x^4
e^x = 1 + x + ----- + ----- + --------- + ...
                2     3 * 2   4 * 3 * 2

             x^2       x^4
cos x = 1 - ----- + --------- - ...
              2     4 * 3 * 2

             x^3         x^5
sin x = x - ----- + ------------- - ...
            3 * 2   5 * 4 * 3 * 2
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
represented as infinite streams.  We will represent the series @math{{a_0 +
a_1 x} + {a_2 x^2} + {a_3 x^3 + \dots}} as the stream whose
elements are the coefficients @math{a_0}, @math{a_1}, @math{a_2}, @math{a_3}, @dots{}.

@enumerate a

@item
The integral of the series @math{{a_0 + a_1 x} + {a_2 x^2} + {a_3 x^3 + \dots}} is the series
@displaymath
c + {a_0 x} + {{1 \over 2} a_1 x^2} + {{1 \over 3} a_2 x^3} + {{1 \over 4} a_3 x^4 + \dots,}
@end displaymath
