@node	3.5.4, 3.5.5, 3.5.3, 3.5
@subsection Streams and Delayed Evaluation

The @code{integral} procedure at the end of the preceding section shows how we
can use streams to model signal-processing systems that contain feedback loops.
The feedback loop for the adder shown in @ref{Figure 3.32} is modeled by the
fact that @code{integral}'s internal stream @code{int} is defined in terms of
itself:

@example ocaml
let rec int =
  Cons
    ( initial_value
    , lazy
        (add_streams_float (stream_map (fun x -> dt *. x) integrand) int) )
@end example

@noindent
The ability to deal with such an implicit definition depends on
the promise that is incorporated into @code{cons_stream}.  Without that
promise, OCaml could not construct @code{int} before evaluating
the tail thunk, which would require that @code{int}
already be defined.  In general, delayed evaluation is crucial for using streams to
model signal-processing systems that contain loops.  Without it, our
models would have to be formulated so that the inputs to any signal-processing
component would be fully evaluated before the output could be produced.  This
would outlaw loops.

@noindent
Unfortunately, stream models of systems with loops may require uses of
@code{delay} beyond the ``hidden'' @code{delay} supplied by @code{cons_stream}.
For instance, @ref{Figure 3.34} shows a signal-processing system for solving
the differential equation @math{{dy / dt = f(y)}} where @math{f} is a given
function.  The figure shows a mapping component, which applies @math{f} to its
input signal, linked in a feedback loop to an integrator in a manner very
similar to that of the analog computer circuits that are actually used to solve
such equations.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.34}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.34:} An ``analog computer circuit'' that solves the equation @math{{dy / dt = f(y)}}.

@example
